# Sounds Italian has not got, written for Urdu.
#
# The shape is Italian's own: a phoneme rule sets the source parameters and
# calls one locus rule for the place it is articulated at, where the formant
# targets are. What is here is the locus rules. Which phoneme reaches one is
# a call inside `ital_ph_<name>` in is_val.dr, changed there one line at a
# time, which is how lang/plpl does it too.

# The retroflex locus.
#
# A retroflex is a low third formant -- the tongue tip curled back lengthens
# the cavity in front of the constriction, and F3 comes down towards F2. That
# is the cue, and it is the one thing every description of the series agrees
# on.
#
# Italian's trill is f2 1250 and its dentals are f2 1700, both with a third
# formant left where the vowel had it. These numbers put F2 a little above
# the dentals and bring F3 down to meet it, which is the textbook retroflex
# and is deliberately not what Polish settled on: `pol_retroflex_Fv' is f2
# 1800 and f3 2500, a hair above Italian's palatal, because Polish sz is a
# palato-alveolar that its speakers hear as further back rather than a true
# retroflex. Urdu's are the real thing, so they start where the phonetics
# says and not where Polish's ear landed.
#
# Unheard as they stand. Polish's were arrived at by building three settings
# and listening to five words, and these have had no such hearing yet; when
# they get one this comment should say what was tried and what was kept.
rule urdu_retroflex_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  set f2_in to 1750
  set f2_out to 1750
  set f3_in to 2100
  set f3_out to 2100
  match
end
